dd: add conv=sparse

Add the conv=sparse option known from GNU coreutils dd: an output block
consisting of NUL bytes only is not written but skipped with lseek(), so
that the output file becomes sparse and the skipped blocks do not occupy
any space on filesystems supporting sparse files. If the output ends
with such a hole, the file is extended to its full size with ftruncate()
(only if it is not already larger, e.g. with conv=notrunc).

This allows to write a large, mostly empty disk image from a stream
(e.g. "unzip -p archive.zip disk.img | dd of=disk.img bs=64k conv=sparse")
with considerably less free space than the full image size.

Unlike GNU dd, holes are only created in regular files not opened with
O_APPEND: on a block device a skipped block would keep its old content,
and with O_APPEND (oflag=append, or inherited like ">>" for stdout) all
writes go to the end of the file, so the skipped bytes would be lost.
For other outputs conv=sparse is silently ignored and all blocks are
written.

As the output position might now have been advanced by lseek() for the
last block(s), the final partial output block (ibs != obs) is written
before the optional fsync(), so conv=fsync covers it as well.

Signed-off-by: Jens Maus <mail@jens-maus.de>
Upstream: not yet submitted

